{
  "description": "Certificates in Certificate Transparency logs and the alarms of an implementation that is reachable from outside, SPEC-v0.md section 11.7 item 7 (for a relay also RELAY-v0.md section 6.7). Each case: the names monitored (the host of the public origin; a gateway paired with a relay has one or two public names), the beginning of monitoring (for a relay: the pairing) and the time of the check, the keys agents are shown (the current one, and earlier ones with the time each was replaced), the certificates the administrator has acknowledged (by digest only), and the certificates found in the logs; each with its digest, its names, its validity, the SHA-256 digest of its public key and the time of its first log entry. Result for each certificate, in the order given: alarm (an alarm is raised), past-exposure (the certificate has expired and is reported as a past exposure) or none. A certificate counts for a public name if one of its names matches it without regard to case, or is a wildcard of one label, in the leftmost position, that covers it. A certificate is valid since the beginning of monitoring if its validity, bounds included, overlaps the time from the beginning of monitoring to the check. A certificate of the current key is the implementation's own; one of an earlier key only if it was logged before that key was replaced (strictly). The digest of a certificate stands for the SHA-256 digest of the DER of its TBSCertificate with the poison and the SCT list removed, so that a precertificate and its certificate have one digest; in the cases it is a fixed test value in canonical base64url, one for each certificate and its precertificate. A certificate that is not valid yet (its notBefore is after the time of the check) raises an alarm when its notAfter is not before the time of the check. Public keys stand for the SHA-256 digest of a SubjectPublicKeyInfo and are fixed test values in canonical base64url. Optionally a case also names public keys the administrator has acknowledged (acknowledged_keys: every certificate for one of them counts as the implementation's own) and the key read again from the public origin before an alarm (presented_key: a certificate for it counts as the implementation's own). monitoring: the state of monitoring at the time of the check (SPEC-v0.md section 11.7 item 7, the event ct.status), from the answers of the sources, each with its provider and time: ok if at least two providers answered within the 24 hours before the check, bounds included, limited if one did, unavailable if none did; within the first 24 hours of monitoring the state is ok. identities: certificates in DER (base64) with their identity, the SHA-256 digest of the DER of the TBSCertificate with the poison (OID 1.3.6.1.4.1.11129.2.4.3) and the SCT list (OID 1.3.6.1.4.1.11129.2.4.2) removed from its extensions, in base64url without padding; if no extension remains, the extensions field is removed too. For a precertificate that a precertificate signing certificate issued (RFC 6962 section 3.1), the issuer and the authority key identifier are first replaced by those of the final certificate; no vector covers that.",
